<pretext>
  <docinfo>
    <directories external="external"/>
    <latex-image-preamble><![CDATA[
\usepackage{tikz}
% Redrawn with standard TikZ only; each invocation has its own drawing scope.
\newcommand{\pilotreflection}[3]{%
  \begin{tikzpicture}[x={(1cm,0cm)},y={(.45cm,.3cm)},z={(0cm,1cm)}]
    \foreach \shift/\label in {0/{e_{#1}},4/{U(e_{#1})}} {
      \begin{scope}[xshift=\shift cm]
        \fill[blue!10] (-1,-1,0) -- (1,-1,0) -- (1,1,0) -- (-1,1,0) -- cycle;
        \fill[green!10] (0,-1,-1) -- (0,1,-1) -- (0,1,1) -- (0,-1,1) -- cycle;
        \draw[gray,->] (-1.3,0,0) -- (1.5,0,0) node[right] {$x$};
        \draw[gray,->] (0,-1.3,0) -- (0,1.5,0) node[right] {$y$};
        \draw[gray,->] (0,0,-1.3) -- (0,0,1.5) node[above] {$z$};
        \node at (-.8,-1,0) {$xy$};
        \node at (0,1,-.8) {$yz$};
        \ifnum\shift=0
          \draw[very thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (#2);
        \else
          \draw[very thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (#3);
        \fi
        \node at (0,0,-1.8) {$\label$};
      \end{scope}
    }
    \draw[->] (1.7,0,0) -- (2.7,0,0) node[midway,above] {reflect $xy$};
  \end{tikzpicture}%
}
    ]]></latex-image-preamble>
  </docinfo>
  <article xml:id="pilot-phase1">
    <title>Interactive Linear Algebra: Upstream Fixture</title>
    <section xml:id="pilot-products">
      <title>Products and Composition</title>
      <example xml:id="matrix-mult-eg-mult1">
        <title>A product, column by column</title>
        <p>The nested legacy matrix notation is expanded here using standard matrices. Each column of the product is the first matrix times the corresponding column of the second matrix.</p>
        <p><md number="no">
          \begin{aligned}
          \begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;1\end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\0&amp;1\\1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}
          &amp;=\begin{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}
          &amp;
          \begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}
          \end{pmatrix}\\
          &amp;=\begin{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}&amp;\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}\end{pmatrix}
          =\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\1&amp;1\end{pmatrix}.
          \end{aligned}
        </md></p>
      </example>
      <insight xml:id="pilot-product-sizes">
        <title>The sizes of the matrices in the matrix product</title>
        <p>For <m>AB</m> to be defined, the number of columns of <m>A</m> must equal the number of rows of <m>B</m>. The matching inner dimensions are shown in red; the outer dimensions, shown in blue, give the rows and columns of the result. The positions and labels convey the rule without color.</p>
        <p><md number="no">
          \underbrace{(\textcolor{blue}{m}\times\textcolor{red}{n})}_{A}
          \underbrace{(\textcolor{red}{n}\times\textcolor{blue}{p})}_{B}
          \longrightarrow
          \underbrace{(\textcolor{blue}{m}\times\textcolor{blue}{p})}_{AB}.
        </md></p>
      </insight>
      <paragraphs xml:id="pilot-composition-demo">
        <title>A composition of matrix transformations</title>
        <p>In the interactive, drag the input vector to follow two transformations. A static alternative is given by the formulas
          <md number="no">U(a,b)=(a,b,a),</md>
          <md number="no">T(x,y,z)=(x+y,y+z),</md>
          <md number="no">(T\circ U)(a,b)=(a+b,a+b).</md>
          Thus <m>(1,0)</m> goes first to <m>(1,0,1)</m> and then to <m>(1,1)</m>. The matrices of <m>T</m> and <m>U</m> are the two factors in <xref ref="matrix-mult-eg-mult1"/>.</p>
        <interactive xml:id="pilot-compose3d" iframe="demos/compose3d.html?mat2=1,1,0:0,1,1&amp;mat1=1,0:0,1:1,0&amp;rangeT=off&amp;rangeU=off&amp;rangeTU=off&amp;range=5&amp;closed=true" width="100%" aspect="8:5">
          <shortdescription>Composition from two dimensions to three dimensions and back to two.</shortdescription>
          <static><image xml:id="pilot-compose-static" width="90%">
            <shortdescription>U sends (-1,2) to (-1,2,-1); T sends that vector to (1,1). The spaces have two, three, and two dimensions.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[draw,align=center] (a) at (0,0) {$\mathbb{R}^2$\\$(-1,2)$};
  \node[draw,align=center] (b) at (3,0) {$\mathbb{R}^3$\\$(-1,2,-1)$};
  \node[draw,align=center] (c) at (6,0) {$\mathbb{R}^2$\\$(1,1)$};
  \draw[->] (a) -- node[above] {$U$} (b);
  \draw[->] (b) -- node[above] {$T$} (c);
\end{tikzpicture}
            ]]></latex-image>
          </image></static>
        </interactive>
        <p>The static diagram traces one input; the formulas above describe every input. <url href="https://ulrikbuchholtz.dk/ila/demos/compose3d.html?mat2=1,1,0:0,1,1&amp;mat1=1,0:0,1:1,0&amp;rangeT=off&amp;rangeU=off&amp;rangeTU=off&amp;range=5&amp;closed=true" visual="">Open the composition demo</url>.</p>
      </paragraphs>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="pilot-reflection-projection">
      <title>A Transformation Defined in Steps</title>
      <example xml:id="pilot-reflection-steps">
        <title>Reflect, then project</title>
        <p>Let <m>U</m> reflect across the <m>xy</m>-plane and let <m>T</m> project onto the <m>yz</m>-plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. Then
          <md number="no">U(x,y,z)=(x,y,-z),</md>
          <md number="no">T(x,y,z)=(0,y,z),</md>
          <md number="no">S(x,y,z)=(T\circ U)(x,y,z)=(0,y,-z).</md>
        </p>
        <p>The following three diagrams are editorial redrawings of the legacy reflection pairs, using one shared TikZ helper. The coordinate planes are labeled; the formulas also describe the action without relying on color or images.</p>
        <image xml:id="pilot-reflection-e1" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>Reflection across the xy-plane leaves e1=(1,0,0) fixed; the xy- and yz-planes are labeled in both panels.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>\pilotreflection{1}{1,0,0}{1,0,0}</latex-image>
        </image>
        <p>The first basis vector lies in the reflection plane, but projects to zero:
          <md number="no">U(e_1)=e_1=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{pmatrix},\qquad T(e_1)=S(e_1)=\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\0\end{pmatrix}.</md>
        </p>
        <image xml:id="pilot-reflection-e2" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>Reflection across the xy-plane leaves e2=(0,1,0) fixed; projection onto the yz-plane also fixes this vector.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>\pilotreflection{2}{0,1,0}{0,1,0}</latex-image>
        </image>
        <p>The second basis vector lies in both planes:
          <md number="no">U(e_2)=T(e_2)=S(e_2)=e_2=\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}.</md>
        </p>
        <image xml:id="pilot-reflection-e3" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>Reflection across the xy-plane sends e3=(0,0,1) to (0,0,-1). Both vectors lie in the yz-plane.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>\pilotreflection{3}{0,0,1}{0,0,-1}</latex-image>
        </image>
        <p>The third basis vector is perpendicular to the reflection plane:
          <md number="no">U(e_3)=S(e_3)=-e_3=\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad T(e_3)=e_3=\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}.</md>
        </p>
        <p>These images of the basis vectors give the columns of the matrices:
          <md number="no">B=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\0&amp;0&amp;-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad A=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\0&amp;0&amp;1\end{pmatrix}.</md>
          <md number="no">AB=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;0\\0&amp;0&amp;-1\end{pmatrix}.</md>
        </p>
      </example>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="pilot-row-reduction">
      <title>Row Operations</title>
      <example xml:id="systems-eqns-example1b">
        <title>Solve a system by row operations</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Solve the system
            <md number="no">\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z&amp;=6\\2x-3y+2z&amp;=14\\3x+y-z&amp;=-2.\end{aligned}</md>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>Form the augmented matrix. The vertical line separates coefficients from the right-hand side; each arrow labels a row replacement, not an equation between old and new rows.</p>
          <p><md number="no">
            \begin{aligned}
            \left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;6\\2&amp;-3&amp;2&amp;14\\3&amp;1&amp;-1&amp;-2\end{array}\right)
            &amp;\xrightarrow{R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1}
            \left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;6\\0&amp;-7&amp;-4&amp;2\\3&amp;1&amp;-1&amp;-2\end{array}\right)\\
            &amp;\xrightarrow{R_3\leftarrow R_3-3R_1}
            \left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;6\\0&amp;-7&amp;-4&amp;2\\0&amp;-5&amp;-10&amp;-20\end{array}\right).
            \end{aligned}
          </md></p>
          <p>To obtain a pivot of one without introducing fractions, divide the third row by <m>-5</m>, then swap the second and third rows.</p>
          <p><md number="no">
            \begin{aligned}
            &amp;\xrightarrow{R_3\leftarrow R_3/(-5)}
            \left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;6\\0&amp;-7&amp;-4&amp;2\\0&amp;1&amp;2&amp;4\end{array}\right)\\
            &amp;\xrightarrow{R_2\leftrightarrow R_3}
            \left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;6\\0&amp;1&amp;2&amp;4\\0&amp;-7&amp;-4&amp;2\end{array}\right)\\
            &amp;\xrightarrow{R_3\leftarrow R_3+7R_2}
            \left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;6\\0&amp;1&amp;2&amp;4\\0&amp;0&amp;10&amp;30\end{array}\right)\\
            &amp;\xrightarrow{R_3\leftarrow R_3/10}
            \left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&amp;2&amp;3&amp;6\\0&amp;1&amp;2&amp;4\\0&amp;0&amp;1&amp;3\end{array}\right).
            \end{aligned}
          </md></p>
          <p>The resulting equations are <m>x+2y+3z=6</m>, <m>y+2z=4</m>, and <m>z=3</m>. Back-substitution gives <m>y=-2</m>, then <m>x=1</m>, so <m>(x,y,z)=(1,-2,3)</m>.</p>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <insight xml:id="pilot-row-operation-invariant">
        <title>Row operations preserve solutions</title>
        <p>Doing elementary row operations does not change the solution set of the corresponding linear equations.</p>
      </insight>
      <paragraphs xml:id="pilot-row-demo">
        <title>Explore row reduction</title>
        <p>The local demo starts with the exact system in <xref ref="systems-eqns-example1b"/>. That worked example provides a static alternative, recording every operation and the resulting solution <m>(x,y,z)=(1,-2,3)</m>.</p>
        <interactive xml:id="pilot-rowred1" iframe="demos/rowred1.html" width="100%" aspect="2:1">
          <shortdescription>An interactive row-reduction exercise.</shortdescription>
          <static><image xml:id="pilot-row-static" width="90%">
            <shortdescription>Row reduction sends the given augmented matrix to the identity augmented by (1,-2,3); all steps are worked above.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node (a) at (0,0) {$\left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&2&3&6\\2&-3&2&14\\3&1&-1&-2\end{array}\right)$};
  \node (b) at (5,0) {$\left(\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&0&0&1\\0&1&0&-2\\0&0&1&3\end{array}\right)$};
  \draw[->] (a) -- node[above,align=center] {row\\operations} (b);
\end{tikzpicture}
            ]]></latex-image>
          </image></static>
        </interactive>
        <p><url href="https://ulrikbuchholtz.dk/ila/demos/rowred1.html" visual="">Open the row-reduction demo</url>.</p>
      </paragraphs>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="pilot-bases-vectors">
      <title>Bases and the Number Line</title>
      <definition xml:id="dimension-defn-basis">
        <title>Essential: Basis</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>V</m> be a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>. A <term>basis</term> of <m>V</m> is a list of vectors <m>(v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m)</m> in <m>V</m> such that:
            <ol>
              <li><m>V=\operatorname{Span}\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m\}</m>, and</li>
              <li>the set <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_m\}</m> is linearly independent.</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
      <p>A basis is a minimal spanning set: removing any vector shrinks its span. Every subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> admits a basis.</p>
      <remark xml:id="pilot-ordered-basis">
        <title>Ordered bases</title>
        <p>Whether a list is a basis depends only on its set of vectors. Often that set is called a basis and the list an <em>ordered basis</em>. Order does not affect the abstract basis property, but it matters when computing coordinate vectors and matrices with respect to a basis.</p>
      </remark>
      <remark xml:id="pilot-hidden-content">
        <title>Hidden-content test</title>
        <p>This synthetic remark tests content initially hidden in an HTML knowl by the global publication settings. Its text should be available when the knowl is opened and remain readable in static output.</p>
      </remark>
      <insight xml:id="pilot-basis-cardinality">
        <title>Different bases, the same size</title>
        <p>A nonzero subspace has <em>infinitely many</em> different bases, but they all contain the same number of vectors.</p>
      </insight>
      <warning xml:id="linindep-not-any">
        <title>Not every vector is redundant</title>
        <p>In a linearly dependent set <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}</m>, it is not generally true that <em>any</em> vector <m>v_j</m> is in the span of the others, only that <em>at least one</em> of them is.</p>
        <p>For example, <m>\{(1,0),(2,0),(0,1)\}</m> is linearly dependent because <m>2(1,0)-(2,0)=(0,0)</m>. But <m>(0,1)</m> is not in the span of the other two: every linear combination of <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(2,0)</m> has second coordinate zero.</p>
      </warning>
      <example xml:id="pilot-number-line">
        <title>The number line</title>
        <p>When <m>n=1</m>, we get <m>\mathbb{R}</m> back: <m>\mathbb{R}^1=\mathbb{R}</m>. Geometrically, this is the number line.</p>
        <image xml:id="pilot-number-line-image" width="80%">
          <shortdescription>A horizontal number line with labeled integer ticks from minus three through three and an arrow pointing right.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \draw [->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0);
  \foreach \x in {-3,...,3}
    \draw[thin] (\x,-.1) -- (\x,0.1) node[anchor=south] {$\x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
          ]]></latex-image>
        </image>
      </example>
      <paragraphs xml:id="pilot-vector-demo">
        <title>Vectors as arrows</title>
        <p>The local demo starts with the vector <m>v=(5,3,4)</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. As a static alternative, this is a displacement of five units in the positive <m>x</m> direction, three in the positive <m>y</m> direction, and four in the positive <m>z</m> direction. Drawn from the origin, the arrow ends at <m>(5,3,4)</m>; its double is <m>2v=(10,6,8)</m>.</p>
        <interactive xml:id="pilot-vector" iframe="demos/vector.html" width="100%" aspect="2:1">
          <shortdescription>A three-dimensional vector, initially (5,3,4), displayed geometrically as an arrow.</shortdescription>
          <static><image xml:id="pilot-vector-static" width="65%">
            <shortdescription>Vector from the origin to (5,3,4), with dashed steps of 5 along x, 3 along y, and 4 along z.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(.7cm,0cm)},y={(-.3cm,.3cm)},z={(0cm,.7cm)}]
  \draw[->] (0,0,0) -- (6,0,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[->] (0,0,0) -- (0,4,0) node[above] {$y$};
  \draw[->] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (5,0,0) node[midway,below] {$5$}
    -- (5,3,0) node[midway,below] {$3$} -- (5,3,4) node[midway,right] {$4$};
  \draw[very thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (5,3,4) node[above] {$(5,3,4)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            ]]></latex-image>
          </image></static>
        </interactive>
        <p><url href="https://ulrikbuchholtz.dk/ila/demos/vector.html" visual="">Open the vector demo</url>.</p>
      </paragraphs>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="pilot-subspaces-computation">
      <title>Subspaces and Computation</title>
      <p>For the table, REF means row echelon form and PVF means parametric vector form for the homogeneous solution. The matrix <m>E</m> records the row operations, so <m>EA</m> is in row echelon form.<fn xml:id="pilot-synthetic-footnote">Synthetic migration footnote: this note is newly added to exercise upstream footnote rendering; it is not part of the legacy excerpt.</fn></p>
      <p>The four fundamental subspaces of an <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> of rank <m>r</m>.</p>
      <table xml:id="pilot-four-subspaces">
        <title>The Four Subspaces</title>
        <tabular>
          <col width="16%"/><col width="8%"/><col width="10%" halign="center"/><col width="10%"/><col width="18%"/><col width="16%" halign="center"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor"><cell><p>Subspace</p></cell><cell><p>of</p></cell><cell><p>row/col</p></cell><cell><p>dim</p></cell><cell><p>basis</p></cell><cell><p>changed by row ops?</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><m>\operatorname{Col}(A)</m></p></cell><cell><p><m>\mathbb{R}^m</m></p></cell><cell><p>col</p></cell><cell><p><m>r</m></p></cell><cell><p>pivot cols of <m>A</m></p></cell><cell><p>yes</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><m>\operatorname{Nul}(A)</m></p></cell><cell><p><m>\mathbb{R}^n</m></p></cell><cell><p>row</p></cell><cell><p><m>n-r</m></p></cell><cell><p>vectors in PVF</p></cell><cell><p>no</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><m>\operatorname{Row}(A)</m></p></cell><cell><p><m>\mathbb{R}^n</m></p></cell><cell><p>row</p></cell><cell><p><m>r</m></p></cell><cell><p>nonzero rows of REF</p></cell><cell><p>no</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><m>\operatorname{Nul}(A^T)</m></p></cell><cell><p><m>\mathbb{R}^m</m></p></cell><cell><p>col</p></cell><cell><p><m>m-r</m></p></cell><cell><p>last <m>m-r</m> rows of <m>E</m></p></cell><cell><p>yes</p></cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>The row-picture subspaces <m>\operatorname{Nul}(A)</m> and <m>\operatorname{Row}(A)</m> are unchanged by row operations. The column-picture subspaces <m>\operatorname{Col}(A)</m> and <m>\operatorname{Nul}(A^T)</m> can change.</p>
      <example xml:id="mpp-eg1">
        <title>Floating-point elimination: an inactive-source excerpt</title>
        <p>This static Python listing comes from the currently inactive LU-decomposition source. It attempts to solve <m>10^{-17}x_1+x_2=1</m>, <m>x_1+x_2=2</m> by forcing a small pivot. The original floating-point literals are retained, not replaced by exact rationals.</p>
        <program xml:id="pilot-floating-point-program" language="python" interactive="no"><code><![CDATA[
from sympy import *
# 1e-17 is 10^(-17)
A = Matrix([[1e-17, 1.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0, 2.0]])
# This does R2 -= 10^(17) R1
# (force sympy to use the smaller pivot)
A.row_op(1, lambda v, j: v - 1e17*A[0,j])
pprint(A)
# Now do Jordan substitution
pprint(A.rref(pivots=False))
        ]]></code></program>
        <p>The original recorded output showed a second augmented row <m>(0,-10^{17},-10^{17})</m>, followed by reduced rows <m>(1,0,0)</m> and <m>(0,1,1.0)</m>, suggesting <m>(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)</m>. This output has been verified in native Python 3.12 with SymPy 1.14.0 and in the browser with Pyodide 0.29.3, Python 3.13.2, and bundled SymPy 1.13.3. The listing and results remain available without JavaScript and in PDF; the browser adds an optional executable cell. The exact solution is <m>x_1=1/(1-10^{-17})</m>, <m>x_2=1+1/(1-10^{17})</m>, close to <m>(1,1)</m>.</p>
      </example>
    </section>
    <section xml:id="pilot-determinants">
      <title>Determinant Properties</title>
      <corollary xml:id="pilot-product-invertible">
        <statement><p>Let <m>A_1,A_2,\ldots,A_k</m> be <m>n\times n</m> real matrices. The product <m>A_1A_2\cdots A_k</m> is invertible if and only if each <m>A_i</m> is invertible.</p></statement>
        <proof xml:id="pilot-product-invertible-proof">
          <p>The determinant of a product is the product of its determinants:
            <md number="no">\det(A_1A_2\cdots A_k)=\det(A_1)\det(A_2)\cdots\det(A_k).</md>
            A square matrix is invertible exactly when its determinant is nonzero. The left side is therefore nonzero exactly when the product matrix is invertible. The right side is nonzero exactly when each factor determinant is nonzero, equivalently when each <m>A_i</m> is invertible.</p>
        </proof>
      </corollary>
      <paragraphs xml:id="pilot-multilinearity">
        <title>Multilinearity</title>
        <p>The following observation is useful for theoretical purposes. Regard <m>\det(v_1,\ldots,v_n)</m> as the determinant of the matrix whose rows are <m>v_1,\ldots,v_n</m>.</p>
        <proposition xml:id="det-defn-linear-prop">
          <title>Multilinearity Property</title>
          <statement><p>Fix <m>1\leq i\leq n</m> and all rows other than row <m>i</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>. The map <m>T\colon\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}</m> defined by
            <md number="no">T(x)=\det(v_1,\ldots,v_{i-1},x,v_{i+1},\ldots,v_n)</md>
            is linear.</p></statement>
          <proof xml:id="pilot-multilinearity-proof">
            <p>Editorial shortened proof using cofactor expansion, in place of the legacy row-operation case analysis. Let <m>C_{ij}</m> be the signed cofactor obtained by deleting row <m>i</m> and column <m>j</m>. None of these cofactors depends on the variable row <m>x</m>. Expansion along row <m>i</m> gives
              <md number="no">T(x)=\sum_{j=1}^n x_j C_{ij}.</md>
              For <m>n=1</m>, the empty minor has determinant one, so the same formula applies.</p>
            <p>For any vectors <m>v,w</m> and real scalar <m>c</m>, distributivity in this finite sum proves both defining properties:
              <md number="no">T(v+w)=\sum_{j=1}^n(v_j+w_j)C_{ij}=T(v)+T(w),</md>
              <md number="no">T(cx)=\sum_{j=1}^n c x_j C_{ij}=cT(x).</md>
              Thus <m>T</m> is linear for every choice of row <m>i</m>, not just the first row.</p>
          </proof>
        </proposition>
      </paragraphs>
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>
